{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the city fully wakes. The streets are quiet, the air feels rinsed clean, and even familiar buildings look briefly like ideas rather than facts. A baker unlocks his door, a bus sighs at the curb, and someone in an upstairs window waters plants while still half dreaming. Nothing important is happening, which is exactly why the moment feels so generous. During the day we rush to prove we matter, answering messages, chasing deadlines, turning our attention into a currency we spend too quickly. But dawn asks nothing from us except that we notice it. Maybe that is why I keep a notebook by my bed, not for grand plans, but for small rescued details: the orange scarf of a stranger, rain ticking on a mailbox, the exact shade of light on wet pavement. These fragments rarely become anything useful, yet together they remind me that a life is not built only from milestones. It is also built from pauses, from jokes overheard, from bread shared without ceremony, from walks taken with no destination. If I could give anyone advice, it would be to protect a little useless time each day. Stare out a window, follow a stray thought, let a song finish before moving on. The world will keep demanding urgency, but wonder survives in those who occasionally refuse to hurry. And tomorrow, before the noise begins, I will be there again, listening, grateful, quietly astonished that ordinary mornings can feel miraculous.",
